<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Three permissions this app must never ship, held out of the merged manifest by
      name (BIT-57). All three are enforced rather than trusted because all three can
      arrive without anyone in this repo typing them.

      ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION — the approved map rationale (DEV-54) says bittr needs
      your *approximate* location, matching iOS's kCLLocationAccuracyHundredMeters.
      Map and places SDKs routinely declare FINE in their own manifests, and the
      merger unions permissions: adding one dependency is enough to put "precise
      location" in the merged manifest, on the Play listing's permission list, and
      in the system dialog as a Precise/Approximate toggle — with no Kotlin change
      to review. A source scan cannot see that happen. This line can.

      ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION — the approved reassurance (DEV-54, approved
      2026-09-11) says the location is used "on your device to position the map".
      Collecting it while the map is not on screen is not that. It is also the one
      location permission Play reviews by hand (BIT-51), and geofencing and
      place-awareness SDKs declare it for their own reasons.

      RECORD_AUDIO — the approved scanner rationale (DEV-37) says nothing is
      recorded. CameraX's video artefact (androidx.camera:camera-video) declares
      RECORD_AUDIO, so pulling it in for any reason would advertise a recording
      capability on the store listing even before a line of code used it.

      `tools:node="remove"` is a no-op while nothing declares these, which is the
      point: it is already in place for the merge that would otherwise introduce
      them. If one of them ever becomes genuinely necessary, the copy changes first
      and changed copy goes back through compliance — do not delete these lines to
      make a build pass. Asserted by LocationPrecisionGuardTest,
      LocationEgressGuardTest and CameraCaptureGuardTest.
      RECORD_AUDIO's directive is still a no-op, which is the point: it is already in
      place for the merge that would otherwise introduce it. The location one stopped
      being a no-op at BIT-53 — `org.maplibre.gl:android-sdk` declares
      ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION in its own AAR manifest (verified against the published
      artefact, not its docs), so this line is now the only reason the shipped APK
      does not ask for precise location. That is not a MapLibre wart; every map SDK
      considered did it or recommended it, and it is why the choice was made against
      a test rather than against a comparison table.

      If one of these ever becomes genuinely necessary, the copy changes first and
      changed copy goes back through compliance — do not delete these lines to make a
      build pass. Asserted by LocationPrecisionGuardTest, MapSdkGuardTest and
      CameraCaptureGuardTest.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      The one location permission this app ships (BIT-53, DEV-54). Coarse alone can
      only ever grant approximate location, whatever the user taps in the dialog,
      which is what keeps the shipped map copy true: "To centre the map on where you
      are, bittr needs your approximate location." It is the faithful port of iOS's
      kCLLocationAccuracyHundredMeters (Map/MapViewController.swift:80).

      Declared here rather than left to MapLibre's manifest so that the permission
      bittr does ask for is a decision in this repo, visible in this diff, next to
      the one it refuses.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The network. Declared here rather than inherited (BIT-99).

      Until the Wave 1 screens landed, INTERNET reached the merged manifest only
      through MapLibre's own AAR manifest — so the app's ability to make any request
      at all was a side effect of which map SDK was on the graph. Three screens now
      fetch over the network for reasons of their own: the price chart calls the
      bittr price API, the map syncs BTCMap, and the Academy downloads lesson
      images. A permission three features depend on should be a line in this file.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        The three getbittr.com pages Settings opens in a WebView (Get support, the
        privacy policy, the terms). Also what the price, map and academy screens will
        need — and, later, the Lightning node. BIT-91 records what the app collects;
        this permission does not by itself widen that.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Android 13+ runtime permission for notifications, requested by Settings →
        Device details → Device token, which is the port of iOS's
        `askForPushNotifications()`. The delivery half needs Firebase (BIT-39), which
        only Ruben can provision; asking is the half that works without it, and it is
        exactly what the iOS row does on a device that has not been asked yet.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <application
        android:name=".BittrApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Bittr">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Bittr">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
